---
import { mastheadStamp } from "../../dossiers/headline.js";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import EveryCut from "../../components/EveryCut.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["mnre"];
import { period } from "../../dossiers/periods.js";
import stSolar from "../../../data/series/mnre-states-solar.json";
import stTotal from "../../../data/series/mnre-states.json";
import srcB from "../../../data/series/mnre-sources.json";
import solB from "../../../data/series/mnre-solar-categories.json";
// periods named in the copy, read from the bundles (src/dossiers/periods.js)
const P = period("mnre-sources");
// the plates and every number on the page, computed at build time (src/dossiers/mnre.charts.js)
import * as CH from "../../dossiers/mnre.charts.js";
const N = CH.numbers();
const MONTHS = ["January","February","March","April","May","June","July","August","September","October","November","December"];
const prevShort = (() => { const d = new Date(Date.UTC(+P.year, MONTHS.indexOf(P.month) - 1, 1)); return MONTHS[d.getUTCMonth()].slice(0, 3) + " " + d.getUTCFullYear(); })();
const yearsSince2020 = (() => { const m = (+P.year - 2020) * 12 + MONTHS.indexOf(P.month); const y = Math.floor(m / 12), half = m % 12 >= 6; return `${["zero","one","two","three","four","five","six","seven","eight","nine","ten"][y] ?? y}${half ? " and a half" : ""} years`; })();
// growth multiples named in the copy, computed here (large hydro left out: it entered the count
// only in December 2023, so no growth figure crosses that seam)
const lastV = (b, k) => b.series[k].data.filter((v) => v != null).at(-1);
const firstPos = (b, k) => b.series[k].data.find((v) => v != null && v > 0);
const times = (x) => { const n = Math.round(x); const w = ["", "", "two", "three", "four", "five", "six", "seven", "eight", "nine", "ten", "eleven", "twelve", "thirteen", "fourteen", "fifteen"][n] ?? String(n); return `${Math.abs(n - x) < 0.1 ? "" : n > x ? "nearly " : "more than "}${w}-fold`; };
const exHydro = (lastV(srcB, "grand_total") - lastV(srcB, "large_hydro")) / srcB.series.grand_total.data[0];
const xSolar = times(lastV(srcB, "solar") / firstPos(srcB, "solar"));
const xWindPct = Math.round((lastV(srcB, "wind") / firstPos(srcB, "wind") - 1) * 100);
const xRoof = times(lastV(solB, "rooftop") / firstPos(solB, "rooftop"));
const xGround = times(lastV(solB, "ground_mounted") / firstPos(solB, "ground_mounted"));
const crossYear = (() => { const so = srcB.series.solar.data, w = srcB.series.wind.data; const i = so.findIndex((v, j) => v != null && w[j] != null && v > w[j]); return srcB.dates[i]?.slice(0, 4); })();
const hydroStep = Math.round(srcB.series.large_hydro.data.find((v) => v > 0) / 1000);
const leader = (b) => { const r = Object.entries(b.series).filter(([k]) => !k.includes("__")).map(([, s]) => s).map((s) => ({ name: s.label.split(" · ")[1] ?? s.label, v: s.data.at(-1) })).filter((x) => x.v != null).sort((a, c) => c.v - a.v)[0]; return `${r.name.replace(/\b\w/g, (c) => c.toUpperCase())} (${Math.round(r.v / 1000)} GW)`; };
---
<Base
  title="Renewable Energy · Measure of India"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={mastheadStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="/guides/mnre/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">Once a month the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy counts every megawatt of solar, wind, hydro and biomass capacity installed in the country. Leave out large hydro and the stock has grown {times(exHydro)} since January 2020. Large hydro is the one jump: in December 2023 it added {hydroStep} gigawatts overnight, because the definition changed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>Ministry of New and Renewable Energy</b></span>
      <span>Instrument <b>Installed capacity stock, MW, monthly</b></span>
      <span>Coverage <b>Solar, Wind, Hydro, Bio, Grand Total</b></span>
      <span>Window <b>{P.span}, {P.n} months</b></span>
      <span>Latest <b>{P.short}</b></span>
      <span>Unit <b>MW (shown in GW)</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Grand Total, GW</div><div class="v acc" id="n-total" set:html={N["n-total"]}></div><div class="d" id="d-total" set:html={N["d-total"]}></div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-total"></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Solar, share of total</div><div class="v" id="n-solar-share" set:html={N["n-solar-share"]}></div><div class="d" id="d-solar-share" set:html={N["d-solar-share"]}></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">y/y growth, Grand Total</div><div class="v" id="n-yoy" set:html={N["n-yoy"]}></div><div class="d" id="d-yoy" set:html={N["d-yoy"]}></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">#1 state, GW</div><div class="v" id="n-top-state" set:html={N["n-top-state"]}></div><div class="d" id="d-top-state" set:html={N["d-top-state"]}></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 current reading -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">MNRE, {P.short}</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Grand Total, {P.short}</span><div class="big" id="rb-total" set:html={N["rb-total"]}></div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap">Cumulative installed renewable-energy capacity: every solar panel, wind turbine, hydro dam and biomass plant on the MNRE register. Solar alone is <b id="rt-solar" set:html={N["rt-solar"]}></b>; the four sources together reached this stock in {yearsSince2020} of building.</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk">Solar, GW</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-solar" set:html={N["rb-solar"]}></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the print says</span>
        <p>The {P.long} stock stands at <b id="cr-total" set:html={N["cr-total"]}></b> of installed renewable capacity, up from <b id="cr-total-first" set:html={N["cr-total-first"]}></b> in January 2020. Leave out large hydro, which MNRE began counting only in December 2023, and capacity grew <b id="cr-growth" set:html={N["cr-growth"]}></b> over {yearsSince2020}. Solar is <b id="cr-solar-share" set:html={N["cr-solar-share"]}></b> of the total; wind, <b id="cr-wind-share" set:html={N["cr-wind-share"]}></b>; bio and hydro, the balance.</p>
        <p>Within solar, ground-mounted utility plants hold <b id="cr-gm-share" set:html={N["cr-gm-share"]}></b>; rooftop (including PM Surya Ghar) <b id="cr-rt-share" set:html={N["cr-rt-share"]}></b>. Rooftop grew <b>{xRoof}</b> since the start of the window, faster than any other kind of solar.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <p>It does not say how much electricity was generated. Installed capacity is a stock, the megawatts that exist, not the megawatt-hours that flowed. A solar panel and a wind turbine of the same capacity produce very different units of energy in a year, because the sun and the wind do not blow on the same schedule.</p>
        <p>Nor is the Grand Total one continuous series. In December 2023, large hydro (>25 MW) was folded into the definition, adding ~47 GW in a single month. The step is annotated on every chart that crosses it; growth rates are never computed across the seam.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 register -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The register of series</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{P.short}, vs {prevShort}, since Jan 2020</span></div>
    <div class="break ledger">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Series</th><th>{P.short}</th><th>vs prev. month</th><th>Jan 2020</th><th class="spark">{P.n} months</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="ledger-body" set:html={CH.ledger()}></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 composition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The source mix</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Share of installed capacity, {P.short}</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-comp" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Solar is more than half the room</h3>
            <div class="sub">Share of Grand Total installed capacity by source, All India, {P.short}; large hydro is part of the total, counted since Dec 2023</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-comp" set:html={N["pv-comp"]}></div><div class="c">Solar, share of Grand Total</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-comp" set:html={CH.comp()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, cumulative installed capacity (MW) by source, shares computed from the {P.short} stock, large hydro included (counted since Dec 2023)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The bars show where each installed megawatt comes from. Solar (blue) is the longest: more than half the total. Wind is second. Large hydro, counted only since December 2023, is part of today's total and has its own bar. Bio and small hydro are short bars at the bottom.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 headline : Grand Total -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>Plate 1: the buildout</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Grand Total installed capacity, GW, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-total" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>One rising line, one definitional step</h3>
            <div class="sub">Grand Total renewable capacity, All India, monthly {P.span}, the Dec 2023 step is a classification change (large hydro added), not capacity</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-total" set:html={N["pv-total"]}></div><div class="c">Jan 2020 → {P.short}, the climb</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-total" set:html={CH.total()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, Grand Total installed capacity (MW), monthly, the Dec 2023 vertical rule marks the classification break: large hydro (>25 MW) was folded into the RE total, +48 GW in one month, not a capacity addition, growth is never computed across the seam</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>One rising line: a stock that almost never falls, because capacity is added faster than it retires. Read the slope, not the jumps: the buildout accelerated through 2024-26 as solar capacity was added at record pace. The one violent move is December 2023, where the line steps up by 48 GW: that is not construction, it is the reclassification of large hydro into the renewable total.</p>
            <p>Growth rates computed across the step are meaningless: the series is two different definitions bolted together. The line is drawn as one continuous array (the source publishes it that way) but the seam is annotated so no reader mistakes the step for building.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Grand Total: <b id="ex-total-now" set:html={N["ex-total-now"]}></b>, from <b id="ex-total-first" set:html={N["ex-total-first"]}></b> in Jan 2020. The Dec 2023 step added <b id="ex-total-step" set:html={N["ex-total-step"]}></b>; stripping it out, the underlying buildout added <b id="ex-total-real" set:html={N["ex-total-real"]}></b> in {yearsSince2020}.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 what this measures -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>This guide tracks <b>installed capacity</b>: the megawatts of renewable-energy equipment physically connected to the grid, counted at month-end by the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy. It is a stock, not a flow: it measures what exists, not what it produces. Generation (MWh) depends on capacity factor, how much the sun shines, how hard the wind blows, and is a different statistic entirely.</p>
        <p>Five types are served. <b>Solar</b> splits into ground-mounted utility plants, rooftop (including the PM Surya Ghar residential scheme), hybrid solar-and-other, and off-grid/KUSUM pumps. <b>Wind</b> is a single total. <b>Hydro</b> splits into small (≤25 MW) and large (>25 MW). <b>Bio</b> covers biomass, bagasse cogeneration and waste-to-energy. The <b>Grand Total</b> sums everything the ministry counts as renewable.</p>
        <p>For how renewable power fits the whole energy mix, coal included, and what it means for emissions, see the <a href="/guides/climate/">climate guide</a>, which carries generation and emissions. This guide is about the machines.</p>
        <p>The Grand Total crossed a classification break in December 2023, when large hydro was added to the definition. Before that, the total excluded large hydro (~47 GW); after, it includes it. The two halves are never spliced, and growth is never computed across the seam.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>Capacity ≠ generation.</b> A solar GW and a wind GW produce different MWh in a year. This guide counts megawatts installed, not megawatt-hours delivered.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>The Dec 2023 step.</b> Large hydro (~47 GW) was added to the definition overnight. The Total before and after is not comparable; the seam is annotated on every chart.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>A stock that rarely falls.</b> Capacity is cumulative: added faster than it retires. The line rises; month-on-month declines are rare and small. </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 sources -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>Plate 2: the four sources</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Installed capacity by source, GW, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-sources" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Solar pulled away from everything</h3>
            <div class="sub">Solar, Wind, Small Hydro &amp; Bio, All India, monthly {P.span}, large hydro omitted (enters Dec 2023)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-sources" set:html={N["pv-sources"]}></div><div class="c">Solar ÷ Wind, capacity ratio</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-sources" set:html={CH.sources()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, installed capacity (MW) by source, large hydro omitted from this chart (it enters the definition in Dec 2023 and would dominate the scale), solar grew {xSolar}; wind grew {xWindPct}%; bio and small hydro are nearly flat</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Four lines, four speeds. Solar (blue) grew {xSolar} and overtook wind in {crossYear}. Wind (grey) grew but slowly; bio (grey) and small hydro (grey) are nearly flat. The renewable buildout is, to a first approximation, a solar buildout; the other sources are the base that solar was built on top of.</p>
            <p>Large hydro is deliberately omitted from this chart. It would sit above wind at 47 GW from Dec 2023, but it is a definitional addition, not a buildout. Showing it alongside the genuine growth sources would mislead.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Solar <b id="ex-src-solar" set:html={N["ex-src-solar"]}></b>, Wind <b id="ex-src-wind" set:html={N["ex-src-wind"]}></b>, Bio <b id="ex-src-bio" set:html={N["ex-src-bio"]}></b>, Small Hydro <b id="ex-src-sh" set:html={N["ex-src-sh"]}></b>. Solar is <b id="ex-src-ratio" set:html={N["ex-src-ratio"]}></b> wind's capacity. In Jan 2020 solar was <b id="ex-src-ratio0" set:html={N["ex-src-ratio0"]}></b> wind; the crossover happened inside the window.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 07 solar categories -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>Plate 3: ground vs rooftop</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Solar capacity by sub-category, GW, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-solar" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The rooftop line steepened</h3>
            <div class="sub">Ground Mounted &amp; Rooftop solar (incl. PM Surya Ghar), All India, monthly {P.span}</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-solar" set:html={N["pv-solar"]}></div><div class="c">Rooftop, {P.short}, share of solar</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-solar" set:html={CH.solar()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, solar installed capacity by sub-category, ground mounted is utility-scale; rooftop includes the PM Surya Ghar residential scheme (launched Feb 2024), rooftop grew {xRoof}; ground mounted grew {xGround}</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Two lines, both rising fast. Ground mounted (blue) is the bulk: utility-scale solar parks in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Karnataka. Rooftop (grey) is the surprise: it steepened visibly from 2024 as the PM Surya Ghar scheme pushed residential solar onto household roofs. Rooftop grew {xRoof} across the window; ground mounted grew {xGround}.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Ground mounted <b id="ex-sol-gm" set:html={N["ex-sol-gm"]}></b>, rooftop <b id="ex-sol-rt" set:html={N["ex-sol-rt"]}></b>. Rooftop is <b id="ex-sol-rtshare" set:html={N["ex-sol-rtshare"]}></b> of solar capacity, up from <b id="ex-sol-rtshare0" set:html={N["ex-sol-rtshare0"]}></b> in Jan 2020. Hybrid and off-grid (KUSUM pumps) make up the balance: small but growing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 08 state buildout over time -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>Plate 4: the geography of the buildout</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Top states, Grand Total capacity, GW, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-sttime" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Gujarat and Rajasthan pulled away</h3>
            <div class="sub">Grand Total renewable capacity, top six states, monthly {P.span}, large hydro included from Dec 2023 (states with hydro step up)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-sttime" set:html={N["pv-sttime"]}></div><div class="c">Gujarat + Rajasthan, share of all-India</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-sttime" set:html={CH.sttime()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, Grand Total installed capacity by state, Gujarat (wind + solar) and Rajasthan (solar) out-built every other state; Tamil Nadu plateaus (mature wind base); Karnataka and Himachal step up at Dec 2023 (hydro reclassification)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Six lines, one per state, all rising: but at very different slopes. Gujarat and Rajasthan (blue) pulled away from the pack, together holding roughly a third of national capacity. Gujarat's line is steeper early (wind came first), Rajasthan's steeper later (solar surged). Tamil Nadu plateaus: its wind base was built years ago and grows slowly now.</p>
            <p>The December 2023 step is visible on states with large hydro: Karnataka, Himachal Pradesh and Maharashtra jump where large hydro was added to the definition. Solar-only states (Rajasthan) do not step.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p><span id="ex-st-n1" set:html={N["ex-st-n1"]}></span> <b id="ex-st-gj" set:html={N["ex-st-gj"]}></b>, <span id="ex-st-n2" set:html={N["ex-st-n2"]}></span> <b id="ex-st-rj" set:html={N["ex-st-rj"]}></b>. Together <b id="ex-st-gr2" set:html={N["ex-st-gr2"]}></b> of all-India. The next four, <span id="ex-st-next4n" set:html={N["ex-st-next4n"]}></span>, hold <b id="ex-st-next4" set:html={N["ex-st-next4"]}></b> between them.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-states" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Where the solar is: and where the wind is</h3>
            <div class="sub">Solar capacity by state (top) &amp; Grand Total by state (bottom), {P.short}, the two rankings differ because wind-heavy states fall when only solar is counted</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-states" set:html={N["pv-states"]}></div><div class="c">Rajasthan, solar capacity, #1</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-states" set:html={CH.states()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, Solar Power Total and Grand Total by state/UT, {P.short}, {leader(stSolar)} leads solar; {leader(stTotal)} leads the total, the two rankings differ because wind capacity counts only in the total</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Two strips, one question each. The top strip asks <em>where is the solar?</em> The bottom strip asks <em>where is the total capacity?</em> A state that leads the second without leading the first holds a large wind fleet as well as solar. The rankings swap because wind and solar are built in different geographies: wind on the coasts and passes (Tamil Nadu, Gujarat), solar in the desert (Rajasthan).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Solar #1: <b id="ex-sst-top" set:html={N["ex-sst-top"]}></b>. Total #1: <b id="ex-tst-top" set:html={N["ex-tst-top"]}></b>. The top five states hold <b id="ex-tst-top5" set:html={N["ex-tst-top5"]}></b> of all-India total capacity.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 each source's own map (wave 2) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>Plate 5: each source has its own map</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">State shares of each source's national capacity, {P.short}</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-srcmap" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Seven states hold almost all the wind; the hills hold the hydro; the sugar belt holds the bio power</h3>
            <div class="sub">Each panel ranks the states by their share of one source's national installed capacity, {P.short}. Top eight states shown; every state is in the data download and the table at the end of the guide.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-srcmap" set:html={N["pv-srcmap"]}></div><div class="c">of India's wind is in seven states</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-srcmap" set:html={CH.srcmap()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE installed capacity by state and source, via MoSPI eSankhyiki. Large hydro is plants above 25 MW, counted by MNRE only since December 2023; small hydro is 25 MW and below.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each source is ranked on its own, as a share of its own national total, so a small source and a large one can sit side by side. Read the length of the first few bars: the shorter the list of states that matters, the more a source depends on a particular kind of place. Wind needs steady coastal and plateau winds, large hydro needs mountain rivers, and bio power needs sugar cane, because most of it is burned at sugar mills.</p>
            <p>For a household this is the difference between where power is made and where it is used. Power made in one state travels to others over the national grid, so a state's capacity is not its consumption: a state with no wind farms still uses wind power.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Wind: <b id="ex-sm-wind" set:html={N["ex-sm-wind"]}></b>. Large hydro: <b id="ex-sm-lh" set:html={N["ex-sm-lh"]}></b>. Small hydro: <b id="ex-sm-sh" set:html={N["ex-sm-sh"]}></b>. Bio power: <b id="ex-sm-bio" set:html={N["ex-sm-bio"]}></b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-rooftop" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Rooftop solar is concentrated too: Gujarat and Maharashtra have more than two-fifths of it</h3>
            <div class="sub">Rooftop solar capacity (including the PM Surya Ghar scheme for homes) by state, GW, {P.short}. Top fifteen states.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-rooftop" set:html={N["pv-rooftop"]}></div><div class="c">Gujarat and Maharashtra, share of rooftop solar</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-rooftop" set:html={CH.rooftop()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, rooftop solar (including PM Surya Ghar Yojana) by state, via MoSPI eSankhyiki. Rooftop covers homes, offices and factories alike.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Rooftop panels sit on homes, shops, schools and factories, so this is the solar a person is most likely to see. A state's rooftop capacity depends on its electricity tariffs, how quickly its distribution company connects net meters, and how many roofs it has, not only on sunshine.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Rooftop solar in India: <b id="ex-rt-total" set:html={N["ex-rt-total"]}></b>. <span id="ex-rt-n1" set:html={N["ex-rt-n1"]}></span> <b id="ex-rt-v1" set:html={N["ex-rt-v1"]}></b> and <span id="ex-rt-n2" set:html={N["ex-rt-n2"]}></span> <b id="ex-rt-v2" set:html={N["ex-rt-v2"]}></b> lead; together <b id="ex-rt-share" set:html={N["ex-rt-share"]}></b> of the total.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 bio power (wave 2) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>Plate 6: what bio power burns</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Bio-power capacity by kind, GW, monthly</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-bio" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Most of India's bio power is sugar-mill power, and it has grown slowly</h3>
            <div class="sub">Installed bio-power capacity by kind, GW, monthly {P.span}. Bagasse is the fibre left after crushing sugar cane, burned at the mill for steam and electricity.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-bio" set:html={N["pv-bio"]}></div><div class="c">bagasse cogeneration, share of bio power</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-bio" set:html={CH.bio()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MNRE, bio power by kind (bagasse cogeneration, non-bagasse biomass cogeneration, waste to energy on and off the grid), via MoSPI eSankhyiki. The four kinds add up to the published bio-power total every month.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://mnre.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Four lines, one for each kind of plant. Bagasse cogeneration runs in the crushing season at sugar mills in Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh and Karnataka, which is why bio power follows the sugar belt on the map above. Non-bagasse cogeneration burns other crop and wood residue. Waste to energy burns or digests city and industrial waste; its off-grid line counts plants that power a factory or a campus without feeding the grid.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {P.stamp}</span>
            <p>Bagasse <b id="ex-bio-bag" set:html={N["ex-bio-bag"]}></b>, other biomass <b id="ex-bio-nb" set:html={N["ex-bio-nb"]}></b>, waste to energy <b id="ex-bio-wte" set:html={N["ex-bio-wte"]}></b> on the grid and <b id="ex-bio-off" set:html={N["ex-bio-off"]}></b> off it. Bio power as a whole grew <b id="ex-bio-growth" set:html={N["ex-bio-growth"]}></b> since January 2020, against solar's <b id="ex-bio-solar" set:html={N["ex-bio-solar"]}></b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 how to misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Renewable capacity tripled: India is powered by renewables."</h3>
          <p>Capacity is what is installed; generation is what flows. A solar GW produces perhaps a third of the annual energy of a coal GW, because the sun shines only part of the day. India's grid is still largely thermal; the renewable share of <em>generation</em> is far lower than the share of <em>capacity</em>.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The Grand Total jumped 48 GW in December 2023."</h3>
          <p>It did not. Large hydro (>25 MW) was reclassified into the renewable total that month. No turbine was built; the definition moved. The step is annotated on every chart that crosses it, and growth rates are never computed across the seam.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Rooftop solar means solar on India's homes."</h3>
          <p>The rooftop line counts every roof: factories, offices, schools and shops as well as houses. The PM Surya Ghar scheme for homes is inside it, but MNRE's monthly table does not split homes from businesses, so how much of the rooftop figure sits on houses, this source cannot say.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Rooftop solar is still small: the residential programme failed."</h3>
          <p>Rooftop is a smaller share than ground mounted, but it grew {xRoof} across the window and steepened sharply from 2024 (PM Surya Ghar). Read the slope, not the level: the rooftop line is accelerating faster than any other segment.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Gujarat and Rajasthan lead because they are sunny."</h3>
          <p>Sun helps, but policy and land matter more. Both states offered cheap land, fast approvals and grid connectivity; other equally sunny states (parts of MP, Telangana) did not match them. Capacity follows policy and infrastructure at least as much as it follows irradiance.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

